 1 big thing: Battle of the bots 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images Axios' Ina Fried recently tested two of the newest personal AI agents, OpenAI's Dots and Meta's Muse.

 Ina writes: I asked each assistant to plan a trip to Atlanta to see my beloved WNBA Golden State Valkyries, organize a brunch watch party and create souvenir programs. ☀️ Where they shined: When I asked both agents to find flights and tickets for Game 3 in Atlanta, each caught that the game had been moved up a day and that tickets weren't yet on sale.

 Both found good flight options. 🧐 Where they were creative, but imperfect: For my brunch, Muse designed a colorful 10-page recipe booklet featuring ube waffles and lavender-honey butter. 

 Dots produced a more practical eight-page packet with detailed cooking instructions and attention to dietary preferences. Both designed invitations that needed aesthetic and language edits. 👎 Where they struggled: I asked the agents to help me buy Valkyries trading cards.

 Both helped me compare options and prices. I chose Target, where I had a 5% discount and free shipping. Muse tried to complete the purchase, but got stuck at Target's sign-in page. 

 Dots directed me to the Target app, where my account and payment information were already saved. 💵 The bottom line: Both agents could have done more if I gave them greater access to my accounts and wallet — but I don't trust them enough for that yet.

