 2. 🚀 Cancer meds in space 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The exhaust plume of a SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket is seen this morning from Winter Haven, Fla. Photo: Joe Skipper/Reuters Orbital pharma startup Varda Space Industries launched a mission today to test microgravity's effects on an innovative cancer treatment, Alex Fitzpatrick reports. 

 Developing medicine in space, away from the pesky influence of Earth's gravity, could unlock better treatments — though commercial scale for "space meds" is still a moonshot. Varda's W-1 vehicle after landing in 2024. Photo: John Kraus/Varda 🛰️ The California company's W-7 spacecraft launched into orbit from Cape Canaveral, Florida, on a SpaceX Falcon 9 rideshare mission.

 Varda is testing whether the key ingredient in next-gen cancer medicines called PROTACs will crystallize differently in microgravity than it does on Earth — possibly making the treatment easier for recipients' bodies to absorb. For missions like this one, the company can create "seed quantities" in space, then return the results to Earth to be propagated, Varda chief strategy officer Michael Reilly tells Axios. Go deeper . 

 
 
